  ([38;5;153mgetTrackingId(storage = window.localStorage)[39m), and the file ends with a dual export — a browser global plus
  [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m guarded by [38;5;153mtypeof module !== 'undefined'[39m. That's what lets Node's test runner load the same file the
  page loads, with no build step and no duplicate implementation.

  [1mData flow.[0m Page loads [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m, then [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. On submit, the handler calls [38;5;153mgetTrackingId()[39m and passes it as a third
  positional argument: [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m. [38;5;153mlogin[39m logs it and will include it in the eventual POST to
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m. Future forms call [38;5;153mgetTrackingId()[39m the same way — the id is fetched at use site, not held in a variable,
  so there's no initialization ordering to get wrong.

  [1mError handling[0m — this is where the real work is, and why it isn't a one-line change:

  - [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m access [1mthrows[0m rather than returning null when storage is disabled or blocked. Both read and write are
    wrapped; on failure the module falls back to an in-memory id that lives for the page load. Tracking degrades to
    per-page-load; login never breaks.
  - [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m is undefined outside secure contexts — over [38;5;153mfile://[39m or plain [38;5;153mhttp://[39m it simply isn't there.
    Fallback chain: [38;5;153mrandomUUID[39m → [38;5;153mcrypto.getRandomValues[39m → [38;5;153mMath.random[39m. The last is not cryptographically strong, which
    I'm accepting deliberately: this is an anonymous correlation id, not a credential or a session token. If it ever
    becomes something an authorization decision depends on, that fallback has to go.
  - A stored value that's empty or malformed is discarded and re-minted rather than passed through.

  [1mTesting.[0m Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m — zero dependencies, so [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m stays dependency-free as it is today.
  [38;5;153mtest/tracking.test.js[39m covers: mints and persists on first call; returns the same id on subsequent calls; re-mints when
  the stored value is garbage; survives a storage object whose getter throws; and falls back correctly when [38;5;153mrandomUUID[39m
  is absent. All of it runs against injected fakes, no DOM. Adds [38;5;153m"scripts": { "test": "node --test" }[39m.

  [1mFiles touched:[0m [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m (new), [38;5;153mtest/tracking.test.js[39m (new), [38;5;153mindex.html[39m (one script tag), [38;5;153mapp.js[39m (signature plus the
  call site), [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m (test script).

  [1mOne thing for you, not me, to decide eventually:[0m an anonymous id is still a [3mpersistent identifier[0m, and in some
  jurisdictions that carries consent obligations even without a name attached. Not a blocker and not something I'd build
  now — but worth knowing you've created one before it ships to real users.

  Does this look right? If so I'll write it up as a spec for you to review before any code gets written.

